{ "answer": ["EKG","ECG","Electrocardiogram"], "clues": ["To demonstrate this technology for the first time, British physiologist Augustus Waller used his Samoyed mix named Jimmy. Jimmy was trained to stand patiently with his paws dipped in jars of saline solution.","The first of this machine was built in 1901 and weighed 600 pounds. Kind of like Jimmy, patients had to place both hands and one foot into large buckets of saltwater. Machines now only weigh about 8 pounds","Early versions were labeled with ABCD. Einthoven developed a mathematical formula that compensated for the inertia and friction of the mercury column in the capillary tube and the mathematically corrected reading was subsequently labeled with PQRST."] },
{ "answer": ["Fidaxomicin","dificid"], "clues": ["This drug was initially isolated by Abbott Laboratories in the 1970-80s but they decided to out-license it to PathoGenesis in the 90s. Chiron Corp then acquired PathoGenesis in 2000 but wasn't interested in this medication and sold the licensing rights to a startup, Optimer Pharmaceuticals. Just as Optimer gained FDA approval in 2011, a major compliance scandal emerged and the company imploded. Optimer was bought by Cubist which was then bought by Merck and finally reached the market.","When Abbott Laboratories was developing the drug in the 1980s, the entire pharmaceutical industry was obsessed with finding a cure for MRSA. This drug was fantastic at killing MRSA in test tubes but animal tesing showed that the drug was not absorbed when swallowed.","Because it is barely absorbed, it acts locally in the GI tract and has a narrow spectrum of action. It binds to bacterial RNA polymerase and halts transcription but also uniquely inhibits spore production and toxin formation, lowering the chance of recurrent infection."] },
{ "answer": ["Sciatica"], "clues": ["Hippocrates coined the name of this condition from the Greek word ischios, meaning hip. He thought that symptoms were caused by localized disease or subluxation of the hip joint","The Italian anatomist, Domenico Contugno, proved that this condition is an affliction of the nerve rather than the hip joint. For generations, the condition was widely called Cotugno’s disease. Cotugno also famously discovered cerebrospinal fluid","In 1864, Ernest Lasègue linked low back trouble with leg pain and described the physical mechanics of lifting a straight leg to test for this condition."] },
